<section class="recognition-section reset-frontier" data-editorial id="frontier" aria-labelledby="frontier-title">
  <header class="section-head frontier-head" id="builder-ecosystems">
    <p class="kicker">What we help put in place</p>
    <h2 id="frontier-title">From first use to lasting contribution.</h2>
    <p>Your developers, creators and players already test, build, teach and report problems. We help you choose the one contribution worth supporting, then put the support in place. Each example is one possible focus for a Decision Review or Programme Build.</p>
  </header>
  <ul class="frontier-examples" id="participation" aria-label="Examples of what we help put in place">
    <li><h3>A first useful build</h3><p>When developers sign up, make a few calls and stop, we design the path to a first useful project: a starter task, a place to ask, and a place to show what they made.</p><p class="frontier-practice"><span>In practice</span> <a href="https://nebius.com/blog/posts/introducing-the-nebius-ai-builder-program" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nebius</a> pairs credits with engineer office hours and a showcase route.</p></li>
    <li><h3>A first builder cohort</h3><p>When a few developers already test your models and report what breaks, we decide with you whether a small cohort is worth it. If it is, we build it with you until a first cycle has run and your team owns it.</p><p class="frontier-practice"><span>In practice</span> After its first contributor cohort, <a href="https://zed.dev/blog/zed-guild-retrospective" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zed</a> made later cohorts smaller and more selective so its team would not become the bottleneck.</p></li>
    <li><h3>A route for integrations, tutorials and mods</h3><p>When developers or creators make integrations, tools, tutorials or mods, we set up how that work is submitted, reviewed and found by other users.</p><p class="frontier-practice"><span>In practice</span> <a href="https://docs.fireworks.ai/ecosystem/integrations_process" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fireworks AI</a> reviews community integrations and lists them in its docs. <a href="https://www.fortnite.com/news/build-faster-with-uefn-source-assets-now-on-fab" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Epic</a> lets Fortnite creators license gameplay systems to other creators on Fab.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Contributors who become advocates</h3><p>When people already teach others, host meetups or maintain tools, we define what they receive, what they commit to and how they are recognised. If you already run an ambassador programme, we fix what is unclear and retire what no longer earns its cost.</p><p class="frontier-practice"><span>In practice</span> <a href="https://claude.com/community/ambassadors" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anthropic’s Claude Community Ambassadors</a> receive event funding, API credits and early access, and stay members while they keep hosting.</p></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="frontier-note">“In practice” examples are public programmes from our research. None of these companies are clients of Communitygeeks.</p>
</section>
